{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Your garment did not wear out from upon you, etc.</b> It does not say ״עָלֶיךָ״ but rather ״מֵעָלֶיךָ״. Some say this refers to the perspiration common among wayfarers, for perspiration consumes garments. Therefore it says ״מֵעָלֶיךָ״ — from the thing that is upon you. And accordingly it juxtaposes to it: ״וְרַגְלְךָ לֹא בָצֵקָה״ (and your foot did not swell), for the road is the cause of both. And Rashi explained that the clouds of glory were a covering for their garments. And without doubt, in the place where the clouds of glory, in which the glory of HaShem dwelt, were present, all harmful forces were repelled, for they are not found in the chamber of the Shechinah; therefore he said that they suffered no damage to their garments or to their feet, in the manner that our Sages, of blessed memory, said in tractate Berakhot (5b): 'These garments of the Sages that wear out — it is from their perspiration; these feet that become inflamed — it is from themselves.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase מִנַּיְיהוּ is rendered 'it is from themselves,' understanding it as 'from their own [walking]'; the citation to Berakhot 5b follows the Hebrew reference (ו.) which stands for the sixth folio, corresponding to the Babylonian Talmud Berakhot 5b."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:17.408190599Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}